  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThe omega block begins shifting east Thursday night, and the western \ntrough moves over the Rockies by Friday evening.  Medium range \ndeterministic guidance shows the trough fracturing, and the \nresulting northern stream short wave moves across southern Canada \nand the northern Great Plains.  The cold front attached to the \nsurface reflection of the wave drifts through the Upper Midwest \nSaturday, and slows down to become nearly stationary across southern \nMinnesota, Michigan, and nothwest Iowa on Sunday.  Models then show \nanother upper level wave diving through southwest Canada Sunday \nnight and into the northern Plains Monday where it phases with the \nleft over southern stream energy.  The combined positively tilted \ntrough then digs southeast, which finally pushes the cold front \nthrough the Mid Mississippi Valley on Tuesday.  All this adds up to \ncontinuing dry and warm weather for the Mid Mississippi Valley at \nleast through the weekend south of the cold front, and probably for \nMonday and Tuesday as well.  The LREF continues to show variance in \nthe strength and position of the northern stream trough \nFriday/Saturday, and the combined trough Sunday through Tuesday. The \nvariance mostly affects locations well north of our forecast area \nthrough the weekend.  Temperature IQRs in the LREF are only 3-4 \ndegrees in our area through Sunday.  However, the IQR increases to \n10 degrees across northeast Missouri and west central Illinois as \nthe combined trough and cold front approach the area on Monday. The \nIQR spreads out to 10-15 degrees across the rest of the area on \nTuesday as the members of the LREF attempt to push the front \nthrough.  The deterministic NBM shows highs dropping from the mid \nand upper 70s on Sunday and Monday into the 60s Tuesday with the \nfront. However, confidence in the forecast remains low early next \nweek as guidance continues to have some issues with the placement of \nthe front.",
